<!doctype html>
{#- The page is English chrome around a text (2026-09-14): its bar, cards, keys and every
    announcement are English, and a root that said lang="he" had a screen reader read
    them all in a Hebrew voice. The direction still follows the text, every source cell
    carries its own `lang`, and the text's language rides on `data-language`. -#}
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}" dir="{{ page_direction }}" data-language="{{ source_language }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>⁨{{ title }}⁩{% if english %} · {{ english }}{% endif %}{% if not standalone %} — {{ section.title }}{% endif %}</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}</style>{% if said %}<style>{{ asset('said.css') }}</style>{% endif %}
{%- if preread %}
<style>{{ asset('preread.css') }}</style>
{% endif %}
{#- The Hebrew face is a hundred kilobytes in the page, and a text in a Latin or Cyrillic
    script has no Hebrew to draw with it (2026-09-13). Every Hebrew-script text carries
    it, Yiddish and Aramaic included. -#}
{% if (source_language or 'he').split('-')[0] in ['he', 'iw', 'yi', 'ji', 'arc'] %}{{ hebrew_face(biblical) }}{% endif %}
<script>{{ asset('durable.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('keep.js') }}</script>
</head>
<body class="reader mode-parallel{% if post %} is-post{% endif %}{% if words %} has-list{% endif %}{% if mark_guessed %} mark-guessed{% endif %}{% if verse_by_verse %} verses{% endif %}">
<header class="bar">
  {#- One calm row (targum-internal#421, David, 2026-10-05): the mark, the title, how much
      of it you know, and on the far side Listen, Aa, print and ⋯. It had grown to about
      fifteen controls on two rows of dark filled pills, and the text started a third of
      the way down the window. Nothing was taken away: what you set once lives in the Aa
      panel, the paper in the print choice, and the rare things behind ⋯. A control a
      text does not have is not drawn, in any of the four places.

      The mark holds the corner on its own, and it is the way back. A mark in the corner
      going home is the oldest convention on the web. `id="home"` is on it, so the reader
      wires it as before: a link only where there is somewhere to go, and carrying the
      key it was served with — and, since 2026-10-07, the text's language, so Learn
      opens in it (design.md §12). -#}
  <a class="bar-brand" id="home" href="/" title="{{ t("reader.page.your-targums", "Your targums") }}" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.targum-home", "targum — Your targums") }}" hidden>
    <svg viewBox="20 12 56 72" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <rect x="20" y="12" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="currentColor"></rect>
      <rect x="54" y="22" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="var(--mark-accent)"></rect>
    </svg>
  </a>
  {#- Off the disk there is no Learn page, so the mark is a mark rather than a dead
      link. Same drawing, no href. -#}
  <span class="bar-brand" id="home-plain" role="img" aria-label="targum">
    <svg viewBox="20 12 56 72" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <rect x="20" y="12" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="currentColor"></rect>
      <rect x="54" y="22" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="var(--mark-accent)"></rect>
    </svg>
  </span>
  <div class="bar-title">
    {% if not standalone %}<a class="up" href="index.html" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ title }}</a>{% else %}<span lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ title }}</span>{% endif %}
    {#- The English beside the Hebrew, its own element so the two directions never share
        a string; gone on a phone, where the row has no room, while the document title
        keeps both. -#}
    {% if english %}<span class="bar-english" lang="{{ english_lang|default('en') }}" dir="ltr">{{ english }}</span>{% endif %}
    {#- How much of this text you can already read. Filled in by the reader from the
        words it has, and hidden outright on a text that carries none. It stays when the
        bar steps back: it is the one figure a reader glances up for. -#}
    <span class="known" id="known" hidden></span>
  </div>
  <div class="controls" dir="ltr">
    {#- Listen, with the recording choice inside it (#421). The bar's own play button,
        and the one that brings the strip at the foot back once it has been put away
        (the strip keeps the step, the speed, Hear first and the file). While playing it
        carries a thin line of where the voice is, and that and the count are what is
        left of the bar when it steps back. Only on a text that has audio. -#}
    {% if spoken_audio %}
    <div class="listen" id="listen" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.listen", "Listen") }}">
      <button type="button" class="listen-play" data-play-scene aria-pressed="false"
              title="{{ t("reader.page.play-and-follow", "Play {what} and follow along", what=spoken_label) }}" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.play-what", "Play {what}", what=spoken_label) }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path class="play" d="M5 3.2 12.8 8 5 12.8Z"></path>
          <path class="pause" d="M5.5 3.8v8.4M10.5 3.8v8.4"></path>
        </svg>
      </button>
      <span class="listen-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="listen-fill"></span></span>
      <span class="listen-clock" dir="ltr" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      {#- Chanted or spoken (targum-internal#412), where an aliyah carries both: inside
          the player, because it says what the player plays. The opening position is the
          first, and the script moves it to whichever the reader chose last. -#}
      {% if voices and voices|length > 1 %}
      <button type="button" class="listen-voice" id="voices-open" data-pop="voices" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="voices"
              title="{{ t("reader.page.which-reading", "Which reading") }}">
        <span class="listen-voice-now" id="voice-now">{% if voices[0].key == "chanted" %}{{ t("reader.page.chanted", "Chanted") }}{% else %}{{ t("reader.page.spoken", "Spoken") }}{% endif %}</span>
        <svg class="caret" viewBox="0 0 8 8" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M1.5 3 4 5.5 6.5 3"></path></svg>
      </button>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- How the picture stands (targum-internal#422): the transcript beside it, or the
        picture large with the line said under it. Kept per reader, like the speed. Only
        on a text that carries a picture, and only while the picture is up; on a phone
        the picture is always above its transcript and the switch is not drawn. The
        controls under the picture play it, so Listen stands down while it is up. -#}
    {% if spoken_video %}
    {#- Two drawings since 2026-10-08 (David, calmer surfaces): the picture with lines
        beside it, and the picture large over its line. -#}
    <div class="film-views" id="film-views" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.film.view", "How the video stands") }}">
      <button type="button" class="film-view" data-film-view="beside" aria-pressed="true" aria-label="{{ t("reader.bar.video-beside", "Video beside the transcript") }}" title="{{ t("reader.film.beside-title", "The transcript beside the video (v)") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="2" y="5" width="9" height="7" rx="1.5"></rect><path d="M13.5 6H18M13.5 9H18M13.5 12H18M13.5 15H16"></path></svg>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="film-view" data-film-view="theatre" aria-pressed="false" aria-label="{{ t("reader.film.theatre", "Theatre") }}" title="{{ t("reader.film.theatre-title", "The video large, the line under it (v)") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="2" y="3.5" width="16" height="10" rx="1.5"></rect><path d="M5 16.5h10"></path></svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    <div class="bar-tools">
      {#- By how often they are pressed (David, 2026-10-08, design.md §12): the speed, the
          one switch of marks a language has, and the view, as drawings with their names
          in a tooltip and for a screen reader. -#}
      {% if spoken_audio %}
      <button type="button" class="bar-tool bar-rate" data-pop="rates" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="rates"
              aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.speed", "Speed") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.speed", "Speed") }}"><span class="film-rate-now" dir="ltr">1×</span></button>
      {% endif %}
      {% if has_nikkud and stress_marks %}
      <button type="button" class="bar-tool marks" data-nikkud-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.stress-marks", "Stress marks") }}"
              title="{{ t("reader.page.stress-marks-n-we-mark-a-word", "Stress marks (n). We mark a word only where two sources agree, so some words stay bare.") }}"><span class="marks-glyph" lang="ru" aria-hidden="true">а́</span></button>
      {% elif has_nikkud %}
      <button type="button" class="bar-tool marks" data-nikkud-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.vowel-points", "Vowel points") }}"
              {%- if mark_guessed %} title="{{ t("reader.page.vowel-points-n-where-this-text-left", "Vowel points (n). Where this text left a word bare, we guessed — about 90% right on modern Hebrew, less on classical. We mark the sentences we guessed.") }}"
              {%- elif source_pointed %} title="{{ t("reader.page.vowel-points-as-this-text-writes-them", "Vowel points, as this text writes them (n)") }}"
              {%- else %} title="{{ t("reader.page.vowel-points-our-best-guess-about-90", "Vowel points, our best guess — about 90% right on modern Hebrew, less on classical (n)") }}"{% endif %}><span class="marks-glyph" lang="he" aria-hidden="true">אָ</span></button>
      {% elif said %}
      <button type="button" class="bar-tool marks" data-said-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="{{ t("said.switch", "As said") }}"
              title="{{ t("said.switch-title", "As said (n). The liaisons every speaker makes, the elisions, and the final letters nobody says.") }}"><span class="marks-glyph said-glyph" aria-hidden="true">/ə/</span></button>
      {% endif %}
      {#- Three ways to lay the text out: beside, under, or the text on its own. The
          drawing is what each does. On one column (a phone) beside is not offered, and
          on a phone the view is a row of ⋯ instead (below). -#}
      <div class="group modes" id="modes" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.view", "View") }}">
        <span class="slide" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        {%- for key, label, title, path in [
          ("parallel", t("reader.bar.beside", "Translation beside the text"), t("reader.page.translation-beside-the-text-p", "Translation beside the text (p)"), '<path d="M2.5 5h6M2.5 9h6M2.5 13H7M11.5 5h6M11.5 9h6M13 13h4.5"></path>'),
          ("inter", t("reader.bar.under", "Translation under each line"), t("reader.page.translation-under-each-line-l", "Translation under each line (l)"), '<path d="M3 4h14M3 12h14"></path><path class="faint" d="M5 7.5h10M5 15.5h10"></path>'),
          ("source", t("reader.bar.alone", "The text on its own"), t("reader.page.the-text-on-its-own-o", "The text on its own (o)"), '<path d="M3 5h14M6 9h11M3 13h14M9 17h8"></path>'),
        ] %}
        <button type="button" data-mode="{{ key }}"{% if loop.first %} class="on"{% endif %} aria-pressed="{{ 'true' if loop.first else 'false' }}" aria-label="{{ label }}" title="{{ title }}">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">{{ path|safe }}</svg>
        </button>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
      {#- Aa: how the text is set, and what stands beside it. -#}
      <button type="button" class="bar-tool aa-open" id="aa-open" data-pop="aa" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="aa"
              aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.text-and-columns", "Text and columns") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.text-and-columns", "Text and columns") }}"><span class="aa-glyph" aria-hidden="true">A<small>a</small></span></button>
      {#- Paper, where a text has a server that sets it: a portion's reader served from the
          corpus (targum-internal#415). The script shows it with the choice inside; any
          other text's edition is still the command line's (§12, 2026-09-27). -#}
      <button type="button" class="bar-tool print-open" id="print-open" data-pop="print" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="print"
              aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.print-or-save-pdf", "Print or save as PDF") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.print-or-save-pdf", "Print or save as PDF") }}" hidden>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M6 7.5V3.5h8v4"></path>
          <rect x="3" y="7.5" width="14" height="6.5" rx="1.5"></rect>
          <path d="M6 12h8v4.5H6z"></path>
        </svg>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="bar-tool more" data-more aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="more" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.more", "More") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.more", "More") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <circle cx="5" cy="10" r="1.1"></circle><circle cx="10" cy="10" r="1.1"></circle><circle cx="15" cy="10" r="1.1"></circle>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {#- The four panels. Each is drawn under its press on a wide window and as a sheet from
      the foot on a phone; one is open at a time, Escape or a press outside closes it,
      and focus goes in when it opens and back to its press when it closes. -#}
  {% if voices and voices|length > 1 %}
  <div class="bar-pop voices-pop" id="voices" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.which-reading", "Which reading") }}" dir="ltr">
    <div class="group recordings" id="recordings" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.which-reading", "Which reading") }}" data-what="{{ t("reader.page.which-reading", "Which reading") }}">
      {%- for v in voices %}
      <button type="button" class="recording-key{% if loop.first %} on{% endif %}" data-recording="{{ v.key }}" aria-pressed="{{ 'true' if loop.first else 'false' }}" title="{{ v.credited }} {{ v.credit }}"><span class="pick-name">{% if v.key == "chanted" %}{{ t("reader.page.chanted", "Chanted") }}{% else %}{{ t("reader.page.spoken", "Spoken") }}{% endif %}</span><span class="pick-meta">{{ v.credit }}</span></button>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
  {% endif %}
  {#- How fast the video plays, from "1× ▾" under the picture (targum-internal#422). One
      of the bar's panels so it is a visit like them: one at a time, focus in and back,
      Escape and a press outside shut it, and a sheet from the foot on a phone. Drawn
      under its own press rather than under the bar (`data-near`). -#}
  {% if spoken_audio or spoken_video %}
  <div class="bar-pop rates-pop" id="rates" role="group" aria-labelledby="rates-head" dir="ltr" data-near>
    <div class="more-head"><b id="rates-head">{{ t("reader.page.speed", "Speed") }}</b><button type="button" data-pop-close="rates" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.close", "Close") }}">×</button></div>
    <div class="film-rates">
      {%- for rate in ["0.5", "0.75", "1", "1.25", "1.5", "2"] %}
      <button type="button" class="film-rate-pick" data-rate="{{ rate }}" aria-pressed="{{ 'true' if rate == '1' else 'false' }}" dir="ltr">{{ rate }}×</button>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {#- Aa (targum-internal#421): plain labelled rows and quiet switches. The choices that
      have a position are words, the live one underlined in leaf; the things that are on
      or off are switches. Only the rows this text has. -#}
  {% set mains = translations|rejectattr("companion")|list %}
  {% set beside_verse = translations|selectattr("companion")|list %}
  <div class="bar-pop aa" id="aa" role="dialog" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.text-and-columns", "Text and columns") }}" dir="ltr">
    <div class="more-head aa-head-bar"><b>{{ t("reader.page.text-and-columns", "Text and columns") }}</b><button type="button" data-pop-close="aa" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.close", "Close") }}">×</button></div>
    {%- if siblings %}
    {#- The same news at another level. One press, and the reader keeps their place in
        the issue: the same section, not the other level's front. -#}
    <div class="aa-row">
      <span class="aa-label">{{ t("reader.page.level", "Level") }}</span>
      <div class="group levels" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.level", "Level") }}" data-what="Level">
        {%- for other in siblings %}
        {%- if other.current %}
        <span class="level here" aria-current="page" title="{{ t("contents.page.written-for", "Written for {figure}", figure=other.figure) }}">{{ other.name }}</span>
        {%- else %}
        <a class="level" href="../../{{ other.folder }}/reader/{{ 'index.html' if standalone else section.filename }}" title="{{ t("contents.page.written-for", "Written for {figure}", figure=other.figure) }}">{{ other.name }}</a>
        {%- endif %}
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>
    {%- endif %}
    {#- Which rendering the translation column draws from, on a text that carries more
        than one (targum-internal#199): named by language where the languages differ and
        by the rendering where they do not; the full name is the title. `data-drawn` is
        the rendering the cells were written with. A rendering with nothing for this
        section is offered disabled, not as a button that empties the column. -#}
    {% if mains|length > 1 %}
    <div class="aa-row">
      <span class="aa-label">{{ t("reader.page.translation", "Translation") }}</span>
      <div class="group renderings" id="translation" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.translation", "Translation") }}" data-what="Translation" data-drawn="{{ drawn_id }}">
        {%- for t in mains %}
        <button type="button" class="rendering{% if t.id == drawn_id %} on{% endif %}" data-translation="{{ t.id }}" aria-pressed="{{ 'true' if t.id == drawn_id else 'false' }}" title="{{ t.name }}"{% if t.id not in covered %} disabled{% endif %}>{{ t.label }}</button>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- Beside the verse (targum-internal#414): the translation, Onkelos, Rashi, each on or
        off on its own, the way the columns of a printed chumash are each there or not.
        Kept per reader and on every text, so Rashi opened in Genesis is open in Exodus.
        The columns' own headers carry a × and a + for the same switches (#421). -#}
    {% if beside_verse %}
    <div class="aa-rule" role="presentation"></div>
    <div class="group companions" id="companions" role="group" aria-labelledby="aa-beside" data-what="Beside the verse">
      <span class="aa-head" id="aa-beside">{{ t("reader.page.beside-the-verse", "Beside the verse") }}</span>
      {#- While shnayim mikra lays the text out its own way the columns stand aside, and
          the switches say so rather than looking pressed and doing nothing (David, on the
          live site, 2026-10-05). A press on one puts the practice down and the column up. -#}
      <span class="aa-held" id="columns-held" hidden>{{ t("reader.page.shown-in-read", "Shown in Read") }}</span>
      {#- Named for its language where it is the only one; beside a switch of several it
          is "Translation", because the switch above says which. -#}
      {%- set main = (mains|selectattr("id", "equalto", drawn_id)|first) or (mains|first) %}
      <button type="button" class="aa-switch companion on" data-companion="translation" data-on="1" aria-pressed="true" title="{{ main.name if main else '' }}"><span class="aa-name">{{ main.label if main and mains|length == 1 else t("reader.page.translation", "Translation") }}</span><span class="sw" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
      {%- for t in beside_verse %}
      <button type="button" class="aa-switch companion{% if t.on %} on{% endif %}" data-companion="{{ t.companion }}" data-translation-id="{{ t.id }}" data-on="{{ 1 if t.on else 0 }}" aria-pressed="{{ 'true' if t.on else 'false' }}" title="{{ t.name }}"><span class="aa-name">{{ t.label }}</span><span class="sw" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- Shnayim mikra (targum-internal#202): a practice, not a layout (David, 2026-10-05).
        Read is the only way the text is laid out; this switch puts the practice on, and
        while it is on it offers the two ways people keep it — verse by verse, or the whole
        section at a time. Drawn only where the source is Hebrew and Onkelos is beside it;
        the script hides the lot where Onkelos has nothing for this section. The Read press
        stays in the group, unseen, as the practice's "off". -#}
    {% if practice %}
    <div class="aa-practice" id="practice-box">
      <div class="aa-rule" role="presentation"></div>
      <button type="button" class="aa-switch practice-switch" id="practice-on" aria-pressed="false" aria-controls="practice-how"
              title="{{ t("reader.page.each-verse-twice-in-hebrew-then-once", "Each verse twice in Hebrew, then once in Onkelos") }}"><span class="aa-name">{{ t("reader.page.shnayim-mikra", "Shnayim mikra") }}</span><span class="sw" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
      <div class="aa-practice-how" id="practice-how" hidden>
        <p class="aa-note">{{ t("reader.page.each-verse-twice-in-hebrew-then-once", "Each verse twice in Hebrew, then once in Onkelos") }}</p>
        <div class="aa-row">
          <div class="group practice" id="practice" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.shnayim-mikra", "Shnayim mikra") }}" data-what="Shnayim mikra">
            <button type="button" class="practice-key on" data-practice="" aria-pressed="true" title="{{ t("reader.page.read-as-usual", "Read as usual") }}" hidden>{{ t("reader.page.read", "Read") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="practice-key" data-practice="verse" aria-pressed="false" title="{{ t("reader.page.each-verse-twice-in-hebrew-then-once", "Each verse twice in Hebrew, then once in Onkelos") }}">{{ t("reader.page.by-verse", "By verse") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="practice-key" data-practice="section" aria-pressed="false" title="{% if practice == "aliyah" %}{{ t("reader.page.whole-aliyah-twice", "The whole aliyah twice in Hebrew, then once in Onkelos") }}{% else %}{{ t("reader.page.whole-chapter-twice", "The whole chapter twice in Hebrew, then once in Onkelos") }}{% endif %}">{% if practice == "aliyah" %}{{ t("reader.page.by-aliyah", "By aliyah") }}{% else %}{{ t("reader.page.by-chapter", "By chapter") }}{% endif %}</button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- The vowels, the stress marks and how French is said are the bar's own press since
        2026-10-08 (David, calmer surfaces); the chanting marks stay here. -#}
    {% if has_taamim %}
    <div class="aa-rule" role="presentation"></div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- The chanting marks, on scripture that carries them. -#}
    {% if has_taamim %}
    <button type="button" class="aa-switch" data-taamim-toggle aria-pressed="true"
            title="{{ t("reader.page.the-chanting-marks-as-the-masorah-wrote", "The chanting marks, as the Masorah wrote them (a)") }}"><span class="aa-name">{{ t("reader.page.chanting-marks", "Chanting marks") }}</span><span class="sw" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
    {% endif %}
    <div class="aa-rule" role="presentation"></div>
    <div class="aa-row group" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.type-size", "Type size") }}" data-what="Type">
      <span class="aa-label">{{ t("reader.page.text-size", "Text size") }}</span>
      <span class="aa-size">
        <button type="button" class="aa-step small" data-type="smaller" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.smaller-type", "Smaller type") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.smaller-type", "Smaller type") }}">A</button>
        <span class="aa-meter" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></span>
        <button type="button" class="aa-step large" data-type="larger" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.larger-type", "Larger type") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.larger-type", "Larger type") }}">A</button>
      </span>
    </div>
    <div class="aa-row group" data-what="{{ t("reader.page.line-spacing", "Line spacing") }}">
      <span class="aa-label">{{ t("reader.page.line-spacing", "Line spacing") }}</span>
      <button type="button" class="aa-step leading" data-type="looser" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.line-spacing", "Line spacing") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.line-spacing", "Line spacing") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M7 3.5h10M7 8h10M7 12.5h10M3 2.5v11M1.5 4 3 2.5 4.5 4M1.5 12 3 13.5 4.5 12"></path></svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {#- How the page is drawn, from ⋯ since 2026-10-08 (David, calmer surfaces): the
        highlight of what is not learned yet, pages or one scroll, and one case at a time.
        Highlighting is only offered on a text with words to mark. -#}
    {% if words %}
    <button type="button" class="aa-switch" data-marking aria-pressed="false"
            title="{{ t("reader.page.highlight-the-words-you-have-not-learned", "Highlight the words you have not learned yet, and clear them as you mark them (m)") }}"><span class="aa-name">{{ t("reader.page.highlight-what-you-have-not-learned", "Highlight what you have not learned") }}</span><span class="sw" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
    {% endif %}
    <button type="button" class="aa-switch" data-paged aria-pressed="false"
            title="{{ t("reader.page.pages-or-one-long-scroll-b", "Pages, or one long scroll (b)") }}"><span class="aa-name">{{ t("reader.page.pages", "Pages") }}</span><span class="sw" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
    {#- One case at a time, on demand. Always-on grammar colouring costs comprehension
        (Lee & Huang 2008), so nothing is shown until a case is chosen, only one is ever
        shown, and it is never remembered (targum-internal#261). -#}
    {% if cases %}
    <div class="aa-row">
      <span class="aa-label">{{ t("reader.page.show-one-case", "Show one case") }}</span>
      <select data-case-lens aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.show-one-case", "Show one case") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.show-every-word-in-one-case-c", "Show every word in one case (c)") }}">
        <option value="">{{ t("reader.bar.case-none", "none") }}</option>
        <option value="Nom">{{ t("reader.bar.case-nom", "nominative") }}</option>
        <option value="Gen">{{ t("reader.bar.case-gen", "genitive") }}</option>
        <option value="Dat">{{ t("reader.bar.case-dat", "dative") }}</option>
        <option value="Acc">{{ t("reader.bar.case-acc", "accusative") }}</option>
        <option value="Ins">{{ t("reader.bar.case-ins", "instrumental") }}</option>
        <option value="Loc">{{ t("reader.bar.case-loc", "prepositional") }}</option>
      </select>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </div>

  {#- Print (targum-internal#415, #421): the week's sheet as a PDF, as the page is when
      pressed — this aliyah, or the whole portion with its haftarah. Set on the box. The
      script shows the group, and the press, only on a portion's reader served from the
      corpus; a haftarah's reader and every other text leave both hidden. -#}
  <div class="bar-pop print-pop" id="print" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="print-head" dir="ltr">
    <p class="pop-head" id="print-head">{{ t("reader.page.print-or-save-pdf", "Print or save as PDF") }}</p>
    <div class="group to-sheet" id="to-sheet" hidden>
      {% if not standalone %}<a class="more-sheet" id="more-sheet-aliyah" download><span class="pick-name">{{ t("reader.page.this-aliyah", "This aliyah") }}</span><span class="pick-meta" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ section.title }}</span></a>{% endif %}
      <a class="more-sheet" id="more-sheet" download><span class="pick-name">{{ t("reader.page.whole-portion-and-haftarah", "Whole portion and haftarah") }}</span><span class="pick-meta" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ title }}</span></a>
      {#- Why a sheet could not be made, in the box's one sentence; the script fills it. -#}
      <p class="sheet-said" id="sheet-said" hidden></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {#- ⋯: the rare things, each a row named by what it says of itself (`data-what`), its
      control at the end, in the order of ReaderMenus (David, 2026-10-08): this text,
      then listening, then the screen and the keys. Drawn under the press on a wide
      window and from the foot on a phone, over the page rather than laying it out again
      (§12, 2026-09-14). -#}
  <div class="bar-more" id="more" role="dialog" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.this-text", "This text") }}" dir="ltr">
    <div class="more-head"><b>{{ t("reader.page.this-text", "This text") }}</b><button type="button" data-more aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.close", "Close") }}">×</button></div>
    {#- The view, on a phone, where the bar has no room for its drawings: the same
        presses, so one idea of which is on. -#}
    <div class="group modes more-modes" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.view", "View") }}" data-what="{{ t("reader.page.view", "View") }}">
      {%- for key, label, path in [
        ("parallel", t("reader.bar.beside", "Translation beside the text"), '<path d="M2.5 5h6M2.5 9h6M2.5 13H7M11.5 5h6M11.5 9h6M13 13h4.5"></path>'),
        ("inter", t("reader.bar.under", "Translation under each line"), '<path d="M3 4h14M3 12h14"></path><path class="faint" d="M5 7.5h10M5 15.5h10"></path>'),
        ("source", t("reader.bar.alone", "The text on its own"), '<path d="M3 5h14M6 9h11M3 13h14M9 17h8"></path>'),
      ] %}
      <button type="button" data-mode="{{ key }}"{% if loop.first %} class="on"{% endif %} aria-pressed="{{ 'true' if loop.first else 'false' }}" aria-label="{{ label }}" title="{{ label }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">{{ path|safe }}</svg>
      </button>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {#- The word list beside the text, which also has its tab at the foot. -#}
    {% if words %}
    <div class="group" data-what="{{ t("reader.page.word-list", "Word list") }}">
      <button type="button" data-toggle="list" aria-controls="list" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.word-list", "Word list") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.word-list", "Word list") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M5.5 4h8M5.5 8h8M5.5 12h8M2.5 4h0M2.5 8h0M2.5 12h0" />
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- Into a playlist (targum-internal#364). Shown by the script only where the page
        arrived over a connection: a playlist is kept on the account. -#}
    <div class="group to-playlist" id="to-playlist" hidden>
      <a class="more-playlist" id="more-playlist" data-title="{{ title }}">{{ t("reader.page.add-to-playlist", "Add to playlist") }}</a>
    </div>
    {#- Talk to targum about this text (2026-09-11). Hidden until the page knows it is
        served: off a disk there is nothing to talk to. -#}
    <div class="group" data-what="{{ t("reader.bar.talk-to-targum", "Talk to targum") }}">
      <button type="button" class="talk-cta" id="talk-open" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="talk-drawer" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.talk-to-targum-about-this-text", "Talk to targum about this text") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.talk-to-targum-about-this-text", "Talk to targum about this text") }}" hidden>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M8 2.5c-3.3 0-6 2.2-6 5 0 1.5.8 2.9 2.1 3.8L3.5 13.5l3-1.3c.5.1 1 .2 1.5.2 3.3 0 6-2.2 6-5s-2.7-4.9-6-4.9z"/></svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {#- Where the video lives, opened at the line in front of the reader. Only where it
        lives somewhere, and not on a post, whose film goes home from the post's head
        (design.md §12, "A post keeps its shape"). No referrer on the way out. -#}
    {% if spoken_home and not (post and post.home) %}
    {#- Only YouTube's address takes a time; any other home opens at the video's start, so
        its control does not promise the line. -#}
    {% set home_says = t("reader.page.open-on-youtube-at-this-line", "Open on YouTube at this line") if spoken_home_timed else t("reader.page.open-on-service", "Open on {service}", service=spoken_home_named) %}
    <div class="group" data-what="{{ t("reader.bar.the-original", "The original") }}">
      <a class="home" data-home="{{ "at" if spoken_home_timed else "" }}" href="{{ spoken_home }}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"
         title="{{ home_says }}" aria-label="{{ home_says }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M8.5 3.5H3a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v7a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h10a1 1 0 0 0 1-1V8.5"/>
          <path d="M7.5 8.5 13.5 2.5M9.5 2.5h4v4"/>
        </svg>
      </a>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- Watch the recording. Only on a text whose import kept its pictures, and on when
        the text opens: a text that carries media opens as its media (§1, §12). The
        strip at the foot carries the same toggle. -#}
    {% if spoken_video %}
    <div class="group" data-what="{{ t("reader.bar.watch-the-recording", "Watch the recording") }}">
      <button type="button" data-video aria-pressed="false"
              title="{{ t("reader.page.the-video", "The video") }}" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.the-video", "The video") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <rect x="2" y="3.5" width="12" height="9" rx="1"/>
          <path d="M6.5 6 10 8 6.5 10Z"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {#- What the strip carried, for while the picture is up and the strip is not
        (targum-internal#422): Hear first, and a step either side of the voice. The
        file and its credit are the recording's row further down. -#}
    <div class="group film-only" data-what="{{ t("reader.page.hear-first", "Hear first") }}">
      <button type="button" class="more-switch more-first" aria-pressed="false"
              title="{{ t("reader.film.hear-first-title", "Press a line to hear it before you see it") }}" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.hear-first", "Hear first") }}"><span class="sw" aria-hidden="true"></span></button>
    </div>
    <div class="group film-only more-step" data-what="{{ t("reader.page.step", "Step") }}">
      <button type="button" class="more-back" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.back", "Back") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.back", "Back") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M11 3.5 5.5 8 11 12.5"></path><path d="M4 3.5v9"></path></svg>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="more-on" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.forward", "Forward") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.forward", "Forward") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 3.5 10.5 8 5 12.5"></path><path d="M12 3.5v9"></path></svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- Hear a silent text (2026-09-10, targum-internal#246): on a section with no
        recording, the one door that makes one — a call to action, so ink (§9) — with
        what it costs in the reader's own credits beside it (§12, 2026-09-23). Drawn only
        while the voice has a price; never on a text that carries audio. -#}
    {% if voice_offer %}
    <div class="group voice-offer" id="voice-offer" data-section="{{ voice_offer.section }}" data-seconds="{{ voice_offer.seconds }}">
      <span class="label">{{ t("reader.page.no-recording", "No recording") }}</span>
      <button type="button" class="voice-go" id="voice-go">{{ t("reader.page.hear-this-section", "Hear this section") }}</button>
      <span class="voice-cost">{{ tn("reader.page.voice-costs-credits", voice_offer.minutes, "This uses about {n} credit", "This uses about {n} credits") }}</span>
      <span class="voice-said" id="voice-said" role="status"></span>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {% if spoken_audio %}
    {#- The strip's one-off actions, on a phone, where the strip has no width left for
        them. Their OWN classes, not the strip's; the script wires both to one handler. -#}
    <div class="group more-player">
      <span class="label">{{ t("reader.page.the-recording", "The recording") }}</span>
      <a class="more-get" download>{{ t("reader.page.save-the-audio", "Save the audio") }}</a>
      {#- Whose recording it is, beside the control that carries it off (#342). -#}
      {% if voices %}{% for v in voices %}<span class="more-credit">{{ v.credited }} {{ v.credit }}{% if v.licence %} · {{ v.licence }}{% endif %}</span>{% endfor %}
      {% elif speech_credit %}<span class="more-credit">{{ speech_credited | default("Read by", true) }} {{ speech_credit }}{% if speech_licence %} · {{ speech_licence }}{% endif %}</span>{% endif %}
      <button type="button" class="more-close">{{ t("reader.page.close-the-player", "Close the player") }}</button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- The browser's own full screen, where it has one. `reader.js` shows the row only
        where the browser can do it (not Safari on a phone). -#}
    <div class="group" data-what="{{ t("reader.page.full-screen", "Full screen") }}" id="fullscreen-group" hidden>
      <button type="button" data-fullscreen aria-pressed="false" title="{{ t("reader.page.full-screen-f", "Full screen (f)") }}" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.full-screen", "Full screen") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2.5 6V2.5H6M10 2.5h3.5V6M13.5 10v3.5H10M6 13.5H2.5V10"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {#- The keys say their word (targum-internal#338): a `?` in a corner reads as help.
        Here the row is already named Keys, so the press keeps the mark. -#}
    <div class="group" data-what="{{ t("reader.page.keys", "Keys") }}">
      <button type="button" data-keys aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="keys" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.keyboard-shortcuts", "Keyboard shortcuts") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.keyboard-shortcuts-2", "Keyboard shortcuts (?)") }}"><span class="keys-word">{{ t("reader.page.keys", "Keys") }}</span><span class="keys-mark" aria-hidden="true">?</span></button>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

{#- The sections, straight from the bar.

    The weekly is one long targum, which is right for reading it through and wrong for
    somebody who does not want the politics. A strip under the bar rather than more
    controls inside it: five Hebrew section names would crowd a toolbar that is already
    a mark, a title, three modes, three levels, three type buttons and two toggles — and
    a row of section names under the masthead is what a newspaper does anyway. -#}
{%- if parts %}
<nav class="parts" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.sections", "Sections") }}">
  {%- for part in parts %}
  <a href="#" data-to="{{ part.id }}" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ part.title }}</a>
  {%- endfor %}
</nav>
{%- endif %}

{#- What a keystroke just did, for anyone who is listening rather than looking. Marking a
    word moves a ring and a number in the far corner; neither is announced, and a reader
    working the queue by keyboard alone got no confirmation of anything at all. -#}
<p class="spoken" id="spoken" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>

{#- The first time, and only the first time: one line that says what to do, and after
    the first word is marked, one line that says what the keys are. A reader who has
    never marked a word in this language sees it; after that it is gone for good. -#}
{#- `dir="ltr"`: the line is English chrome inside a page that takes the text's
    direction, and on an RTL page the bidi algorithm carried its full stop to the
    other end — ".Tap a word to say how well you know it". -#}
{% if words %}<p class="first" id="first" dir="ltr" hidden>{{ t("reader.page.tap-a-word-to-say-how-well", "Tap a word to say how well you know it.") }}</p>{% endif %}
{#- Where a press at the foot of the item before landed the reader (design.md §12, "The foot
    is one block", 2026-09-25): once and briefly, what that press did, with the one Undo
    that takes all of it back. The press left the page, so this is where the Undo can be. -#}
<p class="arrived" id="arrived" dir="ltr" role="status" hidden><span id="arrived-said"></span> <button type="button" id="arrived-undo">{{ t("reader.finish.undo", "Undo") }}</button></p>

{#- The picture and its transcript (targum-internal#422, David, 2026-10-05). Two ways to
    stand, chosen in the bar and kept per reader: Beside — the picture at the left with
    one thin row of controls under it, and the transcript beside it, the line being said
    lit as a verse is when it is chanted; and Theatre — the picture large, the line being
    said under it as a subtitle, its words tappable, with the lines either side faded and
    the transcript one press away. While it plays everything but the picture, the line of
    where the voice is and the subtitle steps back. On a phone the picture stands above
    its controls and the transcript runs under them.

    It replaced a picture in a corner that could be picked up and sized, with a second
    floating player bar under it and a full-screen mode of its own. Full screen is the
    browser's, under ⋯; the strip is the transport only while the picture is put away.

    The `<video>` is the page's one media element when it exists, and never fetched from
    any network: the file stands beside this one, so a folder that travels to a disk keeps
    its picture (design.md §12). `data-src`, not `src`: the page arrived with the serve key
    in its query and the script hands it on. The shape the build measured is `data-film`,
    so an upright film is upright before it has loaded. -#}
{% if spoken_video %}
<div class="video{% if spoken_frame and spoken_frame[1] > spoken_frame[0] %} tall{% endif %}" id="video" hidden role="region" aria-label="{{ t("reader.film.the-video", "The video") }}"{% if spoken_frame %} data-film="{{ spoken_frame[0] }} / {{ spoken_frame[1] }}"{% endif %}>
  <div class="film-frame">
    <video class="video-el" playsinline preload="metadata" data-src="{{ spoken_video }}"></video>
    {#- The picture is the biggest thing on the page to press, so a press on it plays
        and pauses — the same wiring as every other play press (`data-play-scene`). No
        glyph sits on it: the row under it says whether it is playing. -#}
    <button type="button" class="video-tap" data-play-scene aria-pressed="false" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.play-or-pause-the-picture", "Play or pause the picture") }}"></button>
    {#- Theatre's picture, made smaller by its lower edge (David, 2026-10-07; design.md
        §12). Inside the frame, over the picture's foot, so it takes no room: a reader who
        never touches it sees Theatre as it was. Shown only in Theatre on a wide window. -#}
    <div class="film-size" role="separator" tabindex="0" aria-orientation="horizontal" aria-valuemin="30" aria-valuemax="100" aria-valuenow="100" aria-label="{{ t("reader.film.size", "Video size") }}" title="{{ t("reader.film.size-title", "Drag to make the video smaller or larger. Double-click for the full size.") }}"><span class="film-grip" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
  </div>
  {#- One thin row: play, where it is, the line of it, how long, how fast, and the line
      held. Text labels and thin strokes, nothing filled (#421's bar). -#}
  <div class="film-ctl" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.film.controls", "Video controls") }}" dir="ltr">
    <button type="button" class="film-play" data-play-scene aria-pressed="false" aria-label="{{ t("reader.film.play", "Play the video") }}" title="{{ t("reader.film.play-title", "Play and pause (Space)") }}">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <path class="play" d="M5 3.2 12.8 8 5 12.8Z"></path>
        <path class="pause" d="M5.5 3.8v8.4M10.5 3.8v8.4"></path>
      </svg>
    </button>
    <span class="film-at" aria-hidden="true">0:00</span>
    {#- A slider like the strip's, and answering the same keys. The faint band on it is
        the line being said. -#}
    <span class="film-track" role="slider" tabindex="0" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.position", "Position") }}"
          aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="0" aria-valuenow="0" aria-valuetext="0:00"><span class="film-line"></span><span class="film-fill"></span></span>
    <span class="film-length" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="film-tools">
      <button type="button" class="film-rate" data-pop="rates" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="rates"
              aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.speed", "Speed") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.speed", "Speed") }}"><span class="film-rate-now">1×</span><svg class="caret" viewBox="0 0 8 8" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M1.5 3 4 5.5 6.5 3"></path></svg></button>
      <button type="button" class="film-loop" aria-pressed="false" title="{{ t("reader.film.loop-title", "Play the line being said again and again") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 7.2V6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 5.5 4h7.2M10.6 1.9 12.7 4l-2.1 2.1M13 8.8v.7A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 10.5 12H3.3M5.4 14.1 3.3 12l2.1-2.1"></path></svg>
        <span class="film-word film-loop-long">{{ t("reader.film.loop", "Loop this line") }}</span><span class="film-word film-loop-short" aria-hidden="true">{{ t("reader.film.loop-short", "Loop") }}</span>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="film-transcript" aria-pressed="false" aria-controls="reader" title="{{ t("reader.film.transcript-title", "The whole transcript beside the video") }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5.5 4h8M5.5 8h8M5.5 12h8M2.5 4h.01M2.5 8h.01M2.5 12h.01"></path></svg>
        <span class="film-word">{{ t("reader.page.transcript", "Transcript") }}</span>
      </button>
    </span>
  </div>
  {#- What it is and whose, under the controls, in Beside. -#}
  <div class="film-about">
    <p class="film-name" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ title }}</p>
    {% if english %}<p class="film-english" lang="en" dir="ltr">{{ english }}</p>{% endif %}
    <p class="film-source" dir="ltr">
      {%- if speech_credit %}<span>{{ speech_credit }}</span>{% endif %}
      {%- if speech_licence %}<span>{% if speech_licence_url %}<a href="{{ speech_licence_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ speech_licence }}</a>{% else %}{{ speech_licence }}{% endif %}</span>{% endif %}
      {%- if not standalone %}<span>{{ t("reader.film.part", "Part {n} of {count}", n=section.number, count=section_count) }}</span>{% endif %}
      <span class="film-length-said"></span>
      {%- if spoken_home and not (post and post.home) %}<span><a class="film-home" data-home="{{ "at" if spoken_home_timed else "" }}" href="{{ spoken_home }}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">{{ t("reader.film.watch-on", "Watch on {service}", service=spoken_home_named) }}</a></span>{% endif %}
    </p>
  </div>
  {#- The line being said, under the picture, in Theatre. Its words are the transcript's
      own, copied in by the script as the clock reaches them, so they carry the same
      levels and open the same card. The lines either side are there to be glanced at,
      not read, and a screen reader is told the line itself. -#}
  <div class="film-sub" id="film-sub">
    <p class="film-far film-before" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}" aria-hidden="true"></p>
    <div class="film-now" aria-live="off"></div>
    {% if translated %}<p class="film-tr" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}"></p>{% endif %}
    <p class="film-far film-after" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}" aria-hidden="true"></p>
  </div>
  {#- The end of a part in Theatre (David, 2026-10-08; design.md §12, "The end of a part is
      the end of a reading"): Theatre shows no transcript, so the foot that ends every
      text — Done, and mark the words never marked — could not be reached from where a
      film ends. Once the film has played to its end the line under the picture gives way
      to this: what ended, the words here never marked, and the foot itself, moved in by
      the script rather than drawn twice, so it is one press with one Undo. -#}
  <section class="film-end" id="film-end" aria-labelledby="film-end-head" dir="ltr" hidden>
    <h2 class="film-end-head" id="film-end-head">{% if not standalone %}{{ t("reader.film.end-of-part", "End of part {n} of {count}", n=section.number, count=section_count) }}{% else %}{{ t("reader.film.end", "The end") }}{% endif %}</h2>
    <p class="film-end-ask" hidden></p>
    <ul class="film-end-words" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}" hidden></ul>
    <div class="film-end-foot"></div>
  </section>
  {#- The way on to the next part, under the picture (David, 2026-10-07: "at the end of the
      first part of a video I uploaded, there's no button to take me to the next video").
      The pager at the foot of the transcript carried it, and Theatre does not show the
      transcript, so a part played to its end led nowhere. Shown once the voice reaches
      the last line, or the film ends; a plain link, so it works off a disk, and the
      script says beside it whether the part is still being made (design.md §12, "One
      press gets the whole video, a part at a time"). Nothing is played by it: the next
      part opens waiting for its press, like any text. -#}
  {% if following and not standalone %}
  <div class="film-next" id="film-next" dir="ltr">
    <a class="film-next-go" href="{{ following.filename }}">{{ t("reader.film.next-part", "Next part") }}<svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 8h10M9 4l4 4-4 4"></path></svg></a>
    <span class="film-next-said" role="status"></span>
    <button type="button" class="film-next-again" hidden>{{ t("reader.film.next-part-again", "Try again") }}</button>
  </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>
{#- The transcript's heading while it is a panel beside a large picture. -#}
<div class="film-panel-head" id="film-panel-head" dir="ltr" hidden>
  <span>{{ t("reader.page.transcript", "Transcript") }}</span>
  <button type="button" class="film-panel-close" aria-label="{{ t("reader.film.close-transcript", "Close the transcript") }}" title="{{ t("reader.film.close-transcript", "Close the transcript") }}">×</button>
</div>
{#- Beside, the line between the picture and its transcript can be taken and moved (David,
    2026-10-08; design.md §12): a wider picture or more room for the text, kept on this
    device. Shown only Beside on a wide window. -#}
<div class="film-split" id="film-split" role="separator" tabindex="0" aria-orientation="vertical" aria-controls="video" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100" aria-valuenow="50"
     aria-label="{{ t("reader.film.split", "Video and transcript") }}" title="{{ t("reader.film.split-title", "Drag to give the video or the transcript more room. Double-click to put it back.") }}" hidden><span class="film-split-line" aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
{% endif %}
<main id="reader">
{#- Small on purpose. A chapter page is for reading, and an image that competed with the
    text would be a decoration; this is a mark, the size of a stamp, that says which book
    you are in. Its own cover where the chapter has one, the book's where it has not. -#}
{% if plate %}<figure class="plate"><img src="{{ plate }}" alt=""></figure>{% endif %}
{#- A post read as a post (design.md §12, "A post keeps its shape", 2026-09-27;
    targum-internal#159). Its head takes the title's place: the author's own picture in a
    disc, or their first letter where the post gave none; their name and handle; the day.
    The pictures follow in the post's order, a row the reader swipes, each at the shape
    it was posted in and never cropped to a card. Then the caption, which is the rows
    below: the reader's own, tapped and translated like any text. No platform mark, no
    platform colour, no button that cannot act there and no count. Everything is in the
    page as data; the one address is the link home, which the reader must press.

    The picture's width and height ride as attributes, so the row is its right shape
    before a byte of it is decoded — and a served page's policy allows no `style`. -#}
{% if post %}
<section class="post" aria-label="{{ t("reader.post.the-post", "The post") }}">
  <header class="post-head">
    <span class="post-face{% if not post.avatar %} letter{% endif %}" aria-hidden="true">{% if post.avatar %}<img src="{{ post.avatar }}" alt="" width="48" height="48">{% else %}{{ post.letter }}{% endif %}</span>
    <span class="post-who">
      {% if post.name %}<bdi class="post-name" dir="auto">{{ post.name }}</bdi>{% endif %}
      {#- Each piece its own isolate, so a Latin handle and an English date on a Hebrew
          page keep their own order, and a narrow window wraps between them, not inside. -#}
      {#- Facebook gives no handle a reader would know its pages by, only a number, so a
          Facebook video's head carries the name and the day alone. -#}
      {% if post.handle or post.day %}<span class="post-meta">{% if post.handle %}<bdi class="post-handle{% if not post.name %} alone{% endif %}" dir="ltr">@{{ post.handle }}</bdi>{% endif %}{% if post.day %}{% if post.handle %}<span aria-hidden="true">·</span>{% endif %}<time datetime="{{ post.stamp }}" dir="auto">{{ post.day }}</time>{% endif %}</span>{% endif %}
    </span>
    {#- The one way home. A film's stands here too, and the bar's gives way to it. -#}
    {% if post.home %}
    <a class="post-home" href="{{ post.home }}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">{{ t("reader.post.on-service", "On {service}", service=post.home_named) }}</a>
    {% endif %}
  </header>
  {#- Where X showed only part of a thread: said once, plainly, with the way to the rest
      already in the head. Never a guess that it is whole. -#}
  {% if post.more %}<p class="post-more">{{ t("reader.post.thread-in-part", "Some of this thread may be missing. The rest is on {service}.", service=post.home_named) }}</p>{% endif %}
  {% if post.pictures %}
  <div class="post-pictures{% if post.pictures|length > 1 %} several{% endif %}" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="{{ tn("reader.post.pictures", post.pictures|length, "{n} picture", "{n} pictures") }}">
    {%- for picture in post.pictures %}
    <figure class="post-picture"><img src="{{ picture.src }}" width="{{ picture.width }}" height="{{ picture.height }}" alt="{{ picture.alt }}" decoding="async"></figure>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}
</section>
{% endif %}
{#- A chapter nobody has paid for yet. It says so, once, above the text, and offers to
    get it; the source is still there to read and mark. The translation column is left
    out rather than shipped empty — a column of blank paragraphs said nothing about
    why, and read as a page that had broken. -#}
{% if not translated and not standalone %}
<div class="waiting-note" id="waiting-note" dir="ltr" data-chapter="{{ section.number }}"{% if audio_waiting %} data-audio{% endif %}>
  {#- An imported recording's part that nobody has bought is waiting on a transcript,
      not on a translation, and a button that names the wrong work makes a promise the
      click will not keep. -#}
  <p>{% if audio_waiting %}We haven't transcribed this part yet.{% else %}We haven't translated this chapter yet.{% endif %}</p>
  {#- A recording's part has no press since 2026-10-07 (design.md §12, "One press gets the
      whole video, a part at a time"): opening it starts it, the line under says it is
      being made, and the page opens itself when it is. The button is only the way to
      try again after a failure. Off a disk nothing runs, and the line above stands. -#}
  {% if audio_waiting %}<p class="waiting-said" id="waiting-said" role="status"></p>{% endif %}
  <button type="button" id="translate-chapter" data-owed="{% if audio_waiting %}transcript{% else %}translation{% endif %}" hidden>{% if audio_waiting %}{{ t("reader.film.next-part-again", "Try again") }}{% else %}Translate{% endif %}</button>
  {#- What the press spends, beside it and shown with it (copy audit, 2026-09-28): a
      translation none. A part of a recording none either since 2026-10-07: the press on
      its quote took the whole recording's credits, and said so (design.md §12, "One
      press gets the whole video, a part at a time"). "Uses 3 credits" here was a second
      price for something already paid for, and it asked again at every part. -#}
  {%- if not audio_waiting %}
  <span class="waiting-cost" id="waiting-cost" hidden>{{ t("reader.chapter.uses-no-credits", "Uses none of your credits") }}</span>
  {%- else %}
  <span class="waiting-cost" id="waiting-cost" hidden>{{ t("reader.chapter.part-paid", "Already in the credits you confirmed") }}</span>
  {%- endif %}
</div>
{% endif %}
{#- The player: the transport for the bar's ▶ Listen. In the page from the start, put away by
    the script as the page opens, and brought up when the voice starts — Listen, Space, a
    line's own press — then put away again by its × (targum-internal#421, David,
    2026-10-05; design.md §12). It stood here from the moment a dialogue opened until
    then, because a reader who did not know the audio was there would not go looking for
    it; the bar's Listen says so now, and saying it twice was one too many.

    In the corner rather than across the top: a bar over the text is a second toolbar,
    and it moves the reading down to make room for something a reader may never press. -#}
{% if spoken_audio %}
<div class="player" id="player" role="group" aria-label="Play {{ spoken_label }}">
  <button type="button" class="player-play" data-play-scene aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Play {{ spoken_label }}">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <path class="play" d="M5 3.5 12.5 8 5 12.5Z"></path>
      <rect class="stop" x="4.5" y="4.5" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
    </svg>
  </button>
  <span class="player-body">
    <span class="player-said" dir="ltr">{% if spoken_video %}{{ t("reader.page.watch-the-video", "Watch the video") }}{% else %}{{ t("reader.page.listen-to", "Listen to {what}", what=spoken_label) }}{% endif %}</span>
    {#- A control, not a readout. It was drawn as decoration for as long as the only way
        through a recording was to press a line, and a reader who wanted the middle of a
        sentence had nowhere to press. `aria-valuetext` is the clock rather than the
        percentage: "four minutes ten of nine minutes" is where you are, and "forty-six
        percent" is arithmetic about where you are. -#}
    <span class="player-track" role="slider" tabindex="0" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.position", "Position") }}"
          aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="0" aria-valuenow="0" aria-valuetext="0:00"><span class="player-fill"></span></span>
    <span class="player-clock" dir="ltr" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </span>
  {#- A step either side of where the voice has got to. On a text whose recording was
      aligned word by word the step is one word; on a text without, five seconds. The
      label says which, and it is written by the reader rather than here, because only
      the page knows whether its clocks go down to words. Two buttons and not one that
      goes back only: a reader who oversteps has to be able to come back. -#}
  <span class="player-step" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.step", "Step") }}">
    <button type="button" class="player-back" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.back", "Back") }}">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <path d="M11 3.5 5.5 8 11 12.5"></path>
        <path d="M4 3.5v9"></path>
      </svg>
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="player-on" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.forward", "Forward") }}">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <path d="M5 3.5 10.5 8 5 12.5"></path>
        <path d="M12 3.5v9"></path>
      </svg>
    </button>
  </span>
  {#- How fast. A pair rather than one button that cycles: a cycle from 2× comes round to
      0.5×, which is the one step nobody pressing "faster" means. In the card and not the
      bar, because the bar's button is only "play" and the speed is a fact about listening,
      not about the page. The readout is the typed × after a number (design.md §7). -#}
  <span class="player-rate" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.speed", "Speed") }}">
    <button type="button" class="player-slower" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.slower", "Slower") }}">−</button>
    <button type="button" class="player-rate-now" dir="ltr" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.speed", "Speed") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.speed", "Speed") }}">1×</button>
    <button type="button" class="player-faster" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.faster", "Faster") }}">+</button>
  </span>
  {#- Hear first (targum-internal#265): a line pressed while this is on plays with its own
      text hidden and the translation left in place, and the text comes back when the line
      ends. Listening practice in breaking speech into words, not a reading aid. Off every
      time the page opens and never remembered, because it is a way of practising rather
      than a setting; and it is here, on the transport, because a page with no recording
      has no strip and so no control. -#}
  {#- The view, in the foot bar on a phone (board ReaderPhone; design.md §12, 2026-10-09):
      the same presses as the bar's and ⋯'s, so one idea of which is on. Not drawn at a
      desk, where the bar carries them; beside is not offered on one column. -#}
  <span class="player-modes" role="group" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.view", "View") }}">
    {%- for key, label, path in [
      ("parallel", t("reader.bar.beside", "Translation beside the text"), '<path d="M2.5 5h6M2.5 9h6M2.5 13H7M11.5 5h6M11.5 9h6M13 13h4.5"></path>'),
      ("inter", t("reader.bar.under", "Translation under each line"), '<path d="M3 4h14M3 12h14"></path><path class="faint" d="M5 7.5h10M5 15.5h10"></path>'),
      ("source", t("reader.bar.alone", "The text on its own"), '<path d="M3 5h14M6 9h11M3 13h14M9 17h8"></path>'),
    ] %}
    <button type="button" data-mode="{{ key }}"{% if loop.first %} class="on"{% endif %} aria-pressed="{{ 'true' if loop.first else 'false' }}" aria-label="{{ label }}" title="{{ label }}">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">{{ path|safe }}</svg>
    </button>
    {%- endfor %}
  </span>
  <button type="button" class="player-first" aria-pressed="false">{{ t("reader.page.hear-first", "Hear first") }}</button>
  {#- The picture, from the transport that is always on screen. The same control as the
      one in the bar — same `data-video`, so `showVideo` presses both and neither can
      show a state the other does not — and here as well because the bar's copy folds
      into the `···` menu on a phone. Closing the picture with its own × then left a
      reader who had put it away with nothing on screen to bring it back: the way back
      was two taps into a menu they had no reason to open. A control that can be turned
      off has to be turnable on from where it was turned off. -#}
  {% if spoken_video %}
  <button type="button" class="player-video" data-video aria-pressed="false"
          aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.the-video", "The video") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.the-video", "The video") }}">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <rect x="2" y="3.5" width="12" height="9" rx="1"/>
      <path d="M6.5 6 10 8 6.5 10Z"/>
    </svg>
  </button>
  {% endif %}
  {#- A real file, carried in the page already, so saving it costs no request. -#}
  <a class="player-get" download aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.download-the-audio", "Download the audio") }}" title="{{ t("reader.page.download-the-audio", "Download the audio") }}">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <path d="M8 2.5v8"></path>
      <path d="M4.5 7.5 8 11l3.5-3.5"></path>
      <path d="M3 13.5h10"></path>
    </svg>
  </a>
  <button type="button" class="player-close" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.close-the-player", "Close the player") }}">×</button>
</div>
{% endif %}
{% if preread %}
{% include '_preread.html.j2' %}
{% endif %}
{#- A way to hear one line. Drawn once and called twice: a dialogue's turn and a verse of
    a recorded book are the same control over the same audio, and two copies of an inline
    SVG is how the two quietly stop matching. -#}
{%- macro hear(id) -%}
<button type="button" class="say" data-id="{{ id }}" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.play-this-line", "Play this line") }}"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path class="play" d="M5 3.5 12.5 8 5 12.5Z"></path><rect class="stop" x="4.5" y="4.5" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect></svg></button>
{%- endmacro -%}
{% for segment in segments %}
  {#- Under a film, what its author typed: a separate item's text (targum-internal#158,
      rule 7), named once where it starts so it is not read as more of what was said. -#}
  {% if post_caption and segment.id == post_caption %}<p class="post-caption" id="caption">{{ t("reader.post.the-caption", "The caption") }}</p>{% endif %}
  {% if segment.kind.value == 'heading' %}
  {% set level = segment.level|default(2, true) %}
  <div class="pair head{% if segment.id in pointed %} points{% endif %}{% if segment.id in unaccented %} accented{% endif %}{% if segment.id in machine %} machine{% endif %}" data-id="{{ segment.id }}" tabindex="-1">
    <h{{ level }} class="src plain" data-form="plain" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ bare[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</h{{ level }}>
    {% if segment.id in pointed %}
    <h{{ level }} class="src pointed" data-form="pointed" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ pointed[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</h{{ level }}>
    {% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in unaccented %}
    <h{{ level }} class="src unaccented" data-form="unaccented" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ unaccented[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</h{{ level }}>
    {% endif %}
    {% if translated %}<p class="tr{% if primary_commentary %} commented{% endif %}" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}">{{ primary.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(target_direction) }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% elif segment.kind.value == 'byline' %}
  <div class="pair byline{% if segment.id in pointed %} points{% endif %}{% if segment.id in unaccented %} accented{% endif %}{% if segment.id in machine %} machine{% endif %}" data-id="{{ segment.id }}" tabindex="-1">
    <p class="src plain" data-form="plain" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ bare[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% if segment.id in pointed %}
    <p class="src pointed" data-form="pointed" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ pointed[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in unaccented %}
    <p class="src unaccented" data-form="unaccented" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ unaccented[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if translated %}<p class="tr{% if primary_commentary %} commented{% endif %}" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}">{{ primary.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(target_direction) }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% elif segment.kind.value == 'turn' %}
  {#- A line of a dialogue. Who said it sits beside the words rather than inside them, so
      the speaker is never pointed, never counted as vocabulary and never read aloud. The
      play control appears only where that line has a span of the scene's audio: a turn
      with no sound shows none, rather than a button that does nothing. -#}
  <div class="pair said{% if segment.id in spoken %} voiced{% endif %}{% if segment.id in pointed %} points{% endif %}{% if segment.id in unaccented %} accented{% endif %}{% if segment.id in primary_coarse %} coarse{% endif %}{% if segment.id in machine %} machine{% endif %}" data-id="{{ segment.id }}" data-speaker="{{ speakers.get(segment.id, '') }}" tabindex="-1">
    {#- Heard by a screen reader too (2026-09-14): hidden, a dialogue was a list of lines
        with nobody saying them. Still never pointed, counted or read aloud by the voice. -#}
    <span class="who" lang="{{ source_language }}">{{ speakers.get(segment.id, '') }}</span>
    {% if segment.id in spoken %}{{ hear(segment.id) }}{% endif %}
    <p class="src plain" data-form="plain" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ bare[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% if segment.id in pointed %}
    <p class="src pointed" data-form="pointed" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ pointed[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in unaccented %}
    <p class="src unaccented" data-form="unaccented" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ unaccented[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if translated %}<p class="tr{% if primary_commentary %} commented{% endif %}" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}">{{ primary.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(target_direction) }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% else %}
  {#- A verse answers to its address. The row is `#2:1`, which is what a link to Ruth 2:1
      says, and the number stands in the margin the way a printed edition sets it: a
      learner locates a line by chapter and verse and nothing else. The number is a link
      to itself, so the address bar carries the verse and a reader can hand it on. -#}
  {% set at = verses.get(segment.id, '') %}
  {#- A row in another language than the page's says so on its own cells: Daniel turns
      into Aramaic at 2:4 and back at 8:1, and `lang` is how a screen reader and a
      spell-checker are told. Where the language turns, a line above the row names it
      once, in the label's size and ink, so the switch is seen rather than inferred from
      words that have stopped answering to a tap. -#}
  {% set tongue = languages.get(segment.id, source_language) %}
  <div class="pair{% if at %} verse{% endif %}{% if segment.kind.value == 'blockquote' %} quote{% endif %}{% if segment.id in spoken %} voiced{% endif %}{% if segment.id in pointed %} points{% endif %}{% if segment.id in unaccented %} accented{% endif %}{% if segment.id in primary_coarse %} coarse{% endif %}{% if segment.id in machine %} machine{% endif %}{% if segment.id in languages %} unread{% endif %}"{% if at %} id="{{ at }}" data-ref="{{ segment.ref }}"{% endif %} data-id="{{ segment.id }}"{% if segment.id in languages %} data-lang="{{ tongue }}"{% endif %} tabindex="-1">
    {% if segment.id in switches %}<span class="language" lang="en">{{ switches[segment.id] }}</span>{% endif %}
    {% if at %}<a class="verse-number" href="#{{ at }}" aria-label="{{ segment.ref }}" title="{{ segment.ref }}">{{ at.split(':')[1] }}</a>{% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in spoken %}{{ hear(segment.id) }}{% endif %}
    <p class="src plain" data-form="plain" lang="{{ tongue }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ bare[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% if segment.id in pointed %}
    <p class="src pointed" data-form="pointed" lang="{{ tongue }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ pointed[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in unaccented %}
    <p class="src unaccented" data-form="unaccented" lang="{{ tongue }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ unaccented[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if translated %}<p class="tr{% if primary_commentary %} commented{% endif %}" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}">{{ primary.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(target_direction) }}</p>{% endif %}
    {#- The companions, under the verse and its translation, a column each (#414). A
        cell for every companion on every verse, empty where it says nothing, so the
        columns stay columns; a companion that is off is hidden, not left out, so turning
        it on is a class rather than a rebuild. -#}
    {% if companions %}
    <div class="beside">
      {%- for c in companions %}
      <p class="cmp{% if c.commented %} commented{% endif %}{% if segment.id not in c.text %} empty{% endif %}" data-t="{{ c.id }}" data-companion="{{ c.key }}" lang="{{ c.language }}" dir="{{ c.direction }}"{% if not c.on %} hidden{% endif %}><span class="cmp-name" lang="en" dir="ltr">{{ c.label }}</span><span class="cmp-text">{{ c.text.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(c.direction) }}</span></p>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
</main>

{#- Turning the page: back, where you are, forward. Fixed at the foot of the window in
    paged mode and not there at all otherwise. Arrows per reading direction, drawn by
    the stylesheet the way the pager's are. -#}
<div class="turn" id="turn" hidden>
  <button type="button" class="back" data-turn="-1" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.previous-page", "Previous page") }}"></button>
  <span id="page-of" dir="ltr"></span>
  <button type="button" class="forward" data-turn="1" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.next-page", "Next page") }}"></button>
</div>

{#- Where the practice stands, at the foot of the section: which reading of it this is,
    and the press to the next. Filled in and shown by the script, and only while the
    practice is kept by section — or, kept by verse, once every verse is read. -#}
{% if practice %}
<div class="practice-step" id="practice-step" dir="ltr" hidden>
  <span id="practice-said"></span>
  <button type="button" id="practice-next"></button>
</div>
{% endif %}
{#- The foot of a section is one block (design.md §12, "The foot is one block",
    2026-09-25): where you are, one press, one quiet way to press it differently, and what
    comes next. It was three things written on three different days — the playlist's Next,
    the offer of the words never marked, and Done — stacked in the order the code happened
    to append them, so moving on came first and finishing came last.

    The press finishes the section and, where words were never marked, marks them known:
    "Done, and mark 12 words known". Offered, never done for you — the press says what it
    does before it is pressed, and "Done without marking" under it finishes the section
    alone. "Words should be marked as known automatically when I'm done with the article",
    said the first alpha reader; this is still the honest version of that.

    At the foot of every section rather than only the last (targum-internal#173): a
    chapter is a chapter however short, the structure decides and there is no floor.
    Finished, the block inverts to ink with an Undo, as it always did. Inside a playlist
    `list.js` puts where you are above the press and what is next under it, and the press
    becomes Next. English chrome in a page that may run right to left, so it carries its own
    direction. -#}
<div class="foot" id="foot" dir="ltr">
  <div class="finished" id="finished" hidden>
    <span id="done-said" hidden></span>
    <button type="button" class="undo" id="done-undo">{{ t("reader.finish.undo", "Undo") }}</button>
  </div>
  <div class="foot-press" id="foot-press">
    <button type="button" class="foot-go" id="done-mark">{{ t("reader.page.done", "Done") }}</button>
    {% if words %}<button type="button" class="foot-plain" id="done-plain" hidden></button>{% endif %}
  </div>
  <p class="foot-next" id="foot-next" hidden></p>
</div>
{#- And where to go next. The next section first, by name and by how long it is: a reader
    who has just finished something should not have to shop, and the library is a shop.

    Shown once this section is finished, and not before. The pager at the foot already
    carries the next section's name, and two controls a hand's width apart saying the
    same thing is the shape this file calls one control nobody presses. Finished, it is
    not the same thing: it is the answer to what a reader does now.

    A relative address, so it works off a disk — unlike the suggestion below it, which is
    the library's and stays hidden until the script says the page arrived over a
    connection. -#}
{% if following %}
<aside class="next-up here" id="next-up" dir="ltr" hidden>
  <span class="next-up-lead">{{ t("reader.page.read-next", "Up next") }}</span>
  <a class="next-up-link" href="{{ following.filename }}"><bdi lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="auto">{{ following.title }}</bdi></a>
  <span class="next-up-why">{{ t("reader.next.minutes", "{n} min", n=minutes[following.number]) }}</span>
  {#- How many of the next one's words the reader already knows, said once, at the foot of
      the first section they finish (targum-internal#335; design.md §12, "Three moments in
      ten minutes"). Written by the script after the press, never on load, with a count
      targum's own server works out: the page carries no list of anybody's words. -#}
  <span class="next-up-known" id="next-up-known" hidden></span>
  {#- And, in the same answer, the way into Claude and ChatGPT: said once, at the moment
      the reader has words to practise, to a reader the server says has no connection
      (design.md §12, "The connector is met on the way in"). -#}
  <span class="next-up-connect" id="next-up-connect" hidden></span>
</aside>
{% elif suggested %}
<aside class="next-up" id="next-up" dir="ltr" hidden>
  <span class="next-up-lead">{{ t("reader.page.read-next", "Up next") }}{% if suggested.scene %} · {{ suggested.scene }}{% endif %}</span>
  {#- `/open/<id>`, the one door onto a catalogue text (targum-internal#313). It sends a
      reader straight to their copy where they have one and to its row with the offer up
      where they have not — never into a build, which is a press and not a redirect. It
      used to be `/library#<id>`, which marks the row and scrolls to it: right about the
      spending and wrong about everything else, because "it should open that targum
      immediately, and not merely bring me to library".

      `/library/<id>` is a different page — the public one, off unless the shelves are —
      and linking there gave a signed-in reader "not found". -#}
  <a class="next-up-link" href="/open/{{ suggested.id }}"><bdi lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="auto">{{ suggested.title }}</bdi>{% if suggested.english %} <span class="next-up-english" lang="en">· {{ suggested.english }}</span>{% endif %}</a>
  {#- Why this one, and how long it is. The blurb used to stand here, and a blurb says
      what a text is rather than why it is next: an offer nobody explained is a curator's
      opinion (targum-internal#176). The blurb is one tap away on the library row this
      links to. -#}
  {% if suggested.because %}<span class="next-up-why">{{ suggested.because }}{% if suggested.minutes %} · {{ t("reader.next.minutes", "{n} min", n=suggested.minutes) }}{% endif %}</span>{% endif %}
  {#- The same line, for a text from the library (targum-internal#335). -#}
  <span class="next-up-known" id="next-up-known" hidden></span>
  <span class="next-up-connect" id="next-up-connect" hidden></span>
  {#- A way to disagree, and nothing more (targum-internal#233). It draws again from a
      list written into the page — the page fetches nothing — and remembers nothing: a
      refusal the server kept would turn a door into a profile nobody asked to build.
      Rendered only where there is a second pick, so a shelf of two texts does not offer
      to draw for ever. Hidden until the script arms it, like the aside around it. -#}
  {% if suggested_more %}<button type="button" class="next-up-else" id="next-up-else" data-more="{{ suggested_more|e }}" hidden>{{ t("reader.page.something-else", "Something else") }}</button>{% endif %}
</aside>
{% endif %}

{% if not standalone %}
<nav class="pager" data-chapter="{{ section.number }}" data-document="{{ document.content_hash }}"{% if by_part %} data-by-part{% endif %}>
  {% if previous %}<a class="prev" href="{{ previous.filename }}">{{ previous.title }}</a>{% endif %}
  {% if following %}<a class="next" href="{{ following.filename }}" data-next="{{ following.number }}">{{ following.title }}</a>{% endif %}
</nav>
{% endif %}
{#- Who and what made this page, at the foot of the text (targum-internal#342, 2026-09-20).
    These stood at the foot of the keyboard-shortcuts card, which a phone never shows — it
    waits for a key to be pressed — so on the device most readers use, a recording under
    CC BY-SA could be played and saved from a page that never said whose it was. "Naming
    them in a file nobody opens is not naming them"; nor is naming them in a card nobody
    can open. They belong to the text, so they travel with it, at every width, on the
    last page where the pager is. -#}
{% if speech_credit or words_credit or russian_words_credit or pointing_credit or lexicon_credit or said or biblical %}
<aside class="credits" id="credits" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.credits", "Credits") }}">
  {#- Who read it. A recording the library serves is somebody else's work under a licence
      that asks for them to be named, and naming them in a file nobody opens is not
      naming them. The text's own credit is on the library page; this one travels with
      the audio, because the audio is what can be saved and carried off. -#}
  {#- Both, where an aliyah carries two readings: each is somebody's, under its own
      licence, and the one not playing can be switched to and saved all the same. -#}
  {% if voices %}
  {% for v in voices %}
  <p class="credit">{{ v.credited }} {{ v.credit }}{% if v.licence %} · {% if v.licence_url %}<a href="{{ v.licence_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ v.licence }}</a>{% else %}{{ v.licence }}{% endif %}{% endif %}</p>
  {% endfor %}
  {% elif speech_credit %}
  <p class="credit">{{ speech_credited | default("Read by", true) }} {{ speech_credit }}{% if speech_licence %} · {% if speech_licence_url %}<a href="{{ speech_licence_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ speech_licence }}</a>{% else %}{{ speech_licence }}{% endif %}{% endif %}</p>
  {% endif %}
  {#- Said once, here, rather than left for a reader to work out from a wrong card.
      The lemmatiser is trained on modern unpointed Hebrew, so on the Tanakh it is
      guessing at forms the training data barely contains: waw-consecutive, pausal
      forms, archaic suffixes. On a modern text this does not need saying, so it is
      not said. targum-internal #87, which asked for the about page — that page is a
      construction notice now, and the sentence belongs where the words are anyway. -#}
  {#- What read the words, named because its licence asks to be. CC BY 4.0 wants the
      work named and the licence linked wherever the work is used, and a credit in a
      file nobody opens is the same non-naming the reading credit above refuses. -#}
  {% if words_credit %}
  <p class="credit">{{ t("reader.page.dictionary-forms-by", "Dictionary forms by") }} <a href="https://huggingface.co/dicta-il/dictabert-joint" rel="noopener">DICTA</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a></p>
  {% endif %}
  {#- Russian's words, where they were read here rather than by the model (LICENSING.md,
      "Russian"): the three things that read them, and OpenCorpora's dictionary under the
      licence that asks for it to be named. -#}
  {% if russian_words_credit %}
  <p class="credit">{{ t("reader.page.dictionary-forms-by", "Dictionary forms by") }} <a href="https://spacy.io/models/ru" rel="noopener">spaCy</a>, <a href="https://stanfordnlp.github.io/stanza/" rel="noopener">Stanza</a> and pymorphy3 with <a href="https://opencorpora.org" rel="noopener">OpenCorpora</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></p>
  {% endif %}
  {#- And what pointed the words, on the same terms and keyed the same way: to the
      vocalizer that ran, and only where it pointed something the edition had not. -#}
  {% if pointing_credit %}
  <p class="credit">{{ t("reader.page.vowel-points-by", "Vowel points by") }} <a href="https://huggingface.co/dicta-il/dictabert-large-char-menaked" rel="noopener">DICTA</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a></p>
  {% endif %}
  {#- OpenRussian's tables, CC BY-SA 4.0, named on a page that quoted them: an aspect
      partner or a word whose stress moves (targum-internal#259). -#}
  {% if lexicon_credit %}
  <p class="credit">{{ t("reader.page.aspect-partners-and-stress-from", "Aspect partners and stress from") }} <a href="https://en.openrussian.org" rel="noopener">OpenRussian.org</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></p>
  {% endif %}
  {% if said %}
  {#- LGPL-LR, as David decided on 2026-09-14: a page that shows Morphalou's readings names
      it and its licence, and says that targum corrects one of them. -#}
  <p class="credit">{{ t("said.credit", "How the French is said, from") }} <a href="https://www.ortolang.fr/market/lexicons/morphalou" rel="noopener">Morphalou 3.1</a>, ATILF CNRS · <a href="https://repository.ortolang.fr/api/content/morphalou/5/licenceLGPLLR.txt" rel="noopener">LGPL-LR</a> · <a href="https://github.com/DLangellotti/targum/blob/master/src/targum/annotate/morphalou_corrections.tsv" rel="noopener">{{ t("said.corrected", "one reading corrected by targum") }}</a></p>
  {% endif %}
  {% if biblical %}
  <p class="credit">{{ t("reader.page.our-dictionary-forms-are-less-reliable-on", "Our dictionary forms are less reliable on biblical Hebrew than
    on modern. The analyser learned from modern unpointed text, so it sometimes misreads
    waw-consecutive, pausal and archaic forms on a card.") }}</p>
  {% endif %}
</aside>
{% endif %}

{% if words %}
<aside class="list" id="list" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.what-you-have-kept-from-this-text", "What you have kept from this text") }}">
  <div class="list-head">
    <div class="list-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.words-or-phrases", "Words or phrases") }}">
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-words" data-list="words" aria-selected="true"
              aria-controls="list-items"><b id="list-count">0</b> <span id="list-count-label">{{ t("reader.page.words", "words") }}</span></button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-phrases" data-list="phrases" aria-selected="false"
              aria-controls="phrase-items"><b id="phrase-count">0</b> <span id="phrase-count-label">{{ t("reader.page.phrases", "phrases") }}</span></button>
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="list-close" data-toggle="list" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.hide-the-list", "Hide the list") }}">×</button>
  </div>
  <p class="list-stats" id="list-stats"></p>
  {#- "Mark them in the text" was here, and it took the colours off and left everything
      else on. The Mark button in the bar does the whole job now. -#}
  <div class="list-body">
    <ol class="list-items" id="list-items" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-words"></ol>
    <p class="list-empty" id="words-empty">{{ t("reader.page.tap-or-click-a-word-to-say", "Tap or click a word to say how well you know it.") }}</p>
    <ol class="list-items" id="phrase-items" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-phrases" hidden></ol>
    <p class="list-empty" id="phrases-empty" hidden>{{ t("reader.page.drag-across-words-to-keep-a-phrase", "Drag across words to keep a phrase.") }}</p>
  </div>
  <div class="list-foot">
    <button type="button" data-export="csv" id="export-button">{{ t("reader.page.export", "Export") }}</button>
    {#- The same list again, shaped for Anki: the pointed word on the front, the sentence
        it was met in on the back. A file most learners already have somewhere to put. -#}
    <button type="button" data-export="anki" id="anki-button">{{ t("reader.page.anki-deck", "Anki deck") }}</button>
  </div>
</aside>
<button type="button" class="list-tab" id="list-tab" data-toggle="list" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="list"><span class="list-tab-said" dir="ltr"><b id="list-tab-count">0</b> <span id="list-tab-label">{{ t("reader.page.words", "words") }}</span><span id="list-tab-phrases" hidden></span></span></button>
{% endif %}

<div id="pick-chip" class="pick-card" hidden></div>
{% if words %}<div id="gloss-card" class="gloss-card" hidden></div>{% endif %}
{#- Before the scripts, not after them. `reader.js` asks for this by id as it loads, and
    for as long as it sat below the script tag the answer was null: the `?` key and the `?`
    button in the bar both went to a card that was never found, so the one page that says
    which keys exist could not be opened by either. -#}
<div class="keys-card" id="keys" hidden role="dialog" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.keyboard-shortcuts", "Keyboard shortcuts") }}">
  {#- Named and closable where a pointer can see it. Escape and a click elsewhere both
      put it away, but nothing on the card said so, and the list beside the text already
      closes with the typed × the guidelines ask for. -#}
  <div class="keys-head"><b>{{ t("reader.page.keys", "Keys") }}</b><button type="button" class="keys-close" data-keys aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.close", "Close") }}">×</button></div>
  {#- In the order a reader reaches for them, not by category: the walk and the levels are
      most of a session, the modes are set once and left. `t` is not here — it reports how
      long the page took to draw, which is for whoever is building targum rather than for
      whoever is reading. -#}
  <dl>
    {% if words %}<dt>&larr; &rarr;</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.forward-through-the-words-you-have-not", "on to the next word you haven't finished with, then the foot of the page, the next page and, after the last, the next chapter; back through every word in order") }}</dd>
    <dt>k 4</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.known", "known") }}</dd>
    <dt>1 2 3</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.just-met-getting-there-nearly-there", "just met, getting there, nearly there") }}</dd>
    <dt>i</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.ignore-it-a-name-a-numeral-a", "ignore it — a name, a numeral, a word from another language") }}</dd>
    <dt>Enter</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.what-the-word-you-are-on-means", "what the word you are on means; again to look it up") }}</dd>
    <dt>u</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.take-back-the-last-level-and-stand", "take back the last level and return to that word") }}</dd>
    <dt>Esc</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.close-this-then-a-word-then-your", "close this, then a word's card, then your saved words, then stop stepping through words") }}</dd>
    <dt>?</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.these-keys", "these keys") }}</dd>
    <dt>{{ t("reader.page.hard", "hard") }}</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.the-level-on-a-word-s-card", "the level on a word's card says how common it is across the language; the number in the library says how much of a text will be new to you") }}</dd>
    <dt>s</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.your-saved-words", "your saved words") }}</dd>
    <dt>m</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.mark-words-as-you-read", "highlight the words you haven't learned") }}</dd>
    <dt>b</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.pages-or-one-long-scroll", "pages, or one long scroll") }}</dd>
    <dt>f</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.full-screen-and-out-of-it", "full screen, and out of it") }}</dd>
    {% if spoken_audio %}<dt>Space</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.key-space", "play {what}, and pause it where it is", what=spoken_label) }}</dd>
    <dt>&lt; &gt;</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.slower-faster", "slower, faster") }}</dd>{% endif %}
    {#- Real keys the card never listed (targum-internal#341, 2026-09-20): the recording's
        track is a slider and answers like one, and the picture's grip and its size handle
        move by the arrows. They work only with the thing itself in focus, and say so. -#}
    {% if spoken_audio %}<dt>Tab ← →</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.keys-on-the-track", "with the recording's track in focus, back or forward five seconds; Home and End jump to its start and end") }}</dd>{% endif %}
    {#- The video's own (targum-internal#422): which way it stands, and a line at a time.
        In Theatre, with no transcript to walk, ← → step lines as well. -#}
    {% if spoken_video %}<dt>v</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.film.key-v", "the transcript beside the video, or the video large") }}</dd>
    <dt>↑ ↓</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.film.key-lines", "the line before, the line after; ← → as well in Theatre") }}</dd>{% endif %}
    <dt>PageDown</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.next-page-pageup-back-on-the-last", "next page; PageUp back. On the last page, the next chapter") }}</dd>
    {% else %}<dt>Esc</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.close-this", "close this") }}</dd>
    <dt>?</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.these-keys", "these keys") }}</dd>{% endif %}
    {% if cases %}<dt>c</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.one-case-at-a-time", "one case at a time") }}</dd>{% endif %}
    {% if said %}<dt>n</dt><dd>{{ t("said.key", "the French as said") }}</dd>{% endif %}
    {% if has_nikkud %}<dt>n</dt><dd>{% if stress_marks %}{{ t("reader.page.key-stress-marks", "stress marks") }}{% else %}{{ t("reader.page.key-vowel-points", "vowel points") }}{% endif %}</dd>{% endif %}
    {% if has_taamim %}<dt>a</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.the-chanting-marks-with-the-vowels-on", "the chanting marks, with the vowels on") }}</dd>{% endif %}
    <dt>p</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.parallel-2", "parallel") }}</dd>
    <dt>l</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.interlinear-the-translation-under-each-line", "interlinear — the translation under each line") }}</dd>
    <dt>o</dt><dd>{{ t("reader.page.source-only-2", "source only") }}</dd>
  </dl>
</div>
{#- Talk to targum, in the reader (2026-09-11): "when I am reading something I want to
    literally be able to chat with it". The same pill and drawer every chrome page has,
    drawn only when the page is served — off a disk there is nothing to talk to — and the
    conversation is told where the reader is: the text, the section, the sentence in
    front of them. Nothing loads until the pill is pressed; the frame has no address
    until then, so the page still fetches nothing by itself. -#}
<div class="talk-scrim" id="talk-scrim" hidden></div>
<aside class="talk-drawer" id="talk-drawer" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.talk-to-targum", "Talk to targum") }}" hidden>
  <div class="talk-drawer-head">
    <span class="talk-drawer-title">{{ t("reader.page.talk-to-targum", "Talk to targum") }}</span>
    <button type="button" class="talk-close" id="talk-close" aria-label="{{ t("reader.page.close", "Close") }}">×</button>
  </div>
  <iframe class="talk-frame" id="talk-frame" title="{{ t("reader.page.talk-to-targum", "Talk to targum") }}" allow="microphone"></iframe>
</aside>
<script type="application/json" id="targum-data">{{ data }}</script>
{#- The ledger's arithmetic, shared with Learn and /progress: the foot of a finished
    section says what moved (targum-internal#175), and the longest run of days is
    counted in one place so three pages cannot disagree about it. -#}
<script>{{ asset('charts.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('vocab.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('sync.js') }}</script>
{#- Saving for offline and the worker that answers it (design.md §12, 2026-10-09). Nothing
    runs off a disk: there is no origin there to keep anything for. -#}
<script>{{ asset('offline.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('events.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('playlist-menu.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('reader.js') }}</script>{% if said %}<script>{{ asset('said.js') }}</script>{% endif %}
{%- if preread %}
<script>{{ asset('preread.js') }}</script>
{% endif %}
{#- A reader opened from a playlist: Next, Back and the swipe (targum-internal#366). Does
    nothing unless the page was served with `?list=`, so a reader off a disk is as it was. -#}
<script>{{ asset('list.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('talk.js') }}</script>
{% if false_friends %}
{#- A French word's false friend on its card (targum-internal#267), only on a page written
    with `TARGUM_FALSE_FRIENDS` on; without it the page ends as it did. #}
<script>{{ asset('false-friends.js') }}</script>
{% endif %}
</body>
</html>
